1
00:00:32,307 --> 00:00:38,307
It's not always easy to deal with what comes naturally.

2
00:01:04,615 --> 00:01:10,615
Animal Behaviour is a comedy, animation film directed by David Fine.

3
00:01:36,923 --> 00:01:42,923
Five animals meet regularly to discuss their inner angst
in a group therapy session led by Dr.

4
00:02:09,230 --> 00:02:15,230
Clement, a canine psychotherapist.

5
00:02:41,538 --> 00:02:47,538
The group includes Lorraine, a leech who suffers from separation anxiety; Cheryl, a praying mantis who can’t seem to keep a man;
Todd, a pig with an eating disorder; Jeffrey, a bird with guilt issues; and Linda, an obsessive-compulsive cat.

6
00:03:13,846 --> 00:03:19,846
Dr. Clement (voice), played by Ryan Beil.

7
00:03:46,153 --> 00:03:52,153
Victor (voice), played by Taz Van Rassel.

8
00:04:18,461 --> 00:04:24,461
Lorraine (voice), played by Leah Juel.

9
00:04:50,769 --> 00:04:56,769
Cheryl (voice), played by Andrea Libman.

10
00:05:23,076 --> 00:05:29,076
Jeffrey (voice), played by James Kirk.

11
00:05:55,384 --> 00:06:01,384
Linda (voice), played by Alison Snowden.

12
00:06:27,692 --> 00:06:33,692
Animal sexual behaviour takes many different forms,
including within the same species.

13
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:06,000
Common mating or reproductively motivated systems include
monogamy, polygyny, polyandry, polygamy and promiscuity.

14
00:07:32,307 --> 00:07:38,307
Other sexual behaviour may be reproductively motivated (e.g.

15
00:08:04,615 --> 00:08:10,615
sex apparently due to duress or coercion and situational sexual
behaviour) or non-reproductively motivated (e.g.

16
00:08:36,923 --> 00:08:42,923
homosexual sexual behaviour, bisexual sexual behaviour, cross-species sex,
sexual arousal from objects or places, sex with dead animals, etc.).

17
00:09:09,230 --> 00:09:15,230
When animal sexual behaviour is reproductively motivated, it is often termed mating or copulation; for most non-human mammals, mating and copulation occur
at oestrus (the most fertile period in the mammalian female's reproductive cycle), which increases the chances of successful impregnation.

18
00:09:41,538 --> 00:09:47,538
Some animal sexual behaviour involves competition,
sometimes fighting, between multiple males.

19
00:10:13,846 --> 00:10:19,846
Females often select males for mating only if they appear
strong and able to protect themselves.

20
00:10:46,153 --> 00:10:52,153
The male that wins a fight may also have the chance to mate with a larger number of
females and will therefore pass on his genes to their offspring.

21
00:11:18,461 --> 00:11:24,461
Historically, it was believed that only humans and a small number of other species performed sexual acts other than for
reproduction, and that animals' sexuality was instinctive and a simple "stimulus–response" behaviour.

22
00:11:50,769 --> 00:11:56,769
However, in addition to homosexual behaviours, a range of species
masturbate and may use objects as tools to help them do so.

23
00:12:23,076 --> 00:12:29,076
Sexual behaviour may be tied more strongly to the establishment and maintenance of complex
social bonds across a population which support its success in non-reproductive ways.

24
00:12:55,384 --> 00:13:01,384
Both reproductive and non-reproductive behaviours can be related to expressions of dominance over another
animal or survival within a stressful situation (such as sex due to duress or coercion).

25
00:13:27,692 --> 00:13:33,692
The tone is lighthearted.
